      "identification": "Museums Victoria: light brown-grey body, tail lightly furred, narrow pale ring around the eye; body up to 11 cm, tail up to 12 cm; small marsupial carnivore with a pointed face and small sharp teeth (https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/species/8425). Australian Museum’s Brown Antechinus page is Antechinus stuartii, not this species; its “Is it a rat?” page says antechinuses have crinkled ears, often notched, and four rows of small sharp incisors rather than one pair of rodent incisors, and shows an image labelled Agile Antechinus without further species text (https://australian.museum/learn/animals/mammals/brown-antechinus/; https://australian.museum/learn/species-identification/ask-an-expert/is-it-a-rat/).",
      "origins": "Family Dasyuridae, order Dasyuromorphia; author Dickman et al., 1998; native to Australia (https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/species/8425). Distribution: south-eastern mainland Australia (https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/species/8425). A finer Victorian range is not stated on the page opened. Inverleigh localities: not stated on sources opened.",
